---
references:
  book:
    id: book
    type: book
    title: A Book
  paper:
    id: paper
    type: article
    title: A Paper
---

§7.3: `citation_number` is stable for a repeated **identity**, and the identity is
whatever `[task.cite] stable_fields` lists — public field paths, before
`field.override` (D-4), in comparison order. `citeX` here produces all four names
listed in the config: `reference_id`, the paren's bare content as `locator_type`,
the paren's quote as `locator_value`, and the extras `"…"` as `author`:

First [^book "p. 1"](paper "ed. 2001")@@citeX{"Smith"} esse do ut anim proident.

The same four values keep the number — only the physical anchor `cite-<n>` is new:

Second [^book "p. 1"](paper "ed. 2001")@@citeX{"Smith"} esse.

One listed field changing is enough for a new number. Here the author differs:

[^book "p. 1"](paper "ed. 2001")@@citeX{"Jones"} esse.

And here `locator_value` does:

[^book "p. 1"](paper "ed. 2002")@@citeX{"Smith"} esse.

`citeY` reads the same `stable_fields`, but it produces only two of them: the
reference comes from the paren's quote and the author from the extras tick, while
`locator_type` and `locator_value` stay absent. An absent field still counts as a
value, so two `citeY` citations match each other — and a `citeY` never matches a
`citeX`, because "no `locator_type`" is not `paper`:

[^]("book")@@citeY{`Smith`} esse.

[^]("book")@@citeY{`Smith`} esse.
